WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:02.969
First up we have a story on talking monkeys.

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:05.920
Why can't monkeys talk?

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:09.060
What would they say if they could?

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:13.220
And most importantly, what would they sound like?

00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:16.680
Dave, did you have an imagined monkey voice

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:18.720
in your head, before hearing the

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:20.060
clip we are about to play?

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:23.720
I guess, Cesear from Planet of the Apes.

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:25.630
Although I know he's not a monkey

00:00:25.630 --> 00:00:28.210
--he is a chimpanzee.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:34.200
Something a little deep, a little guttural, maybe.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:38.520
I had the monkey butler voice in my head,

00:00:38.530 --> 00:00:41.360
The smooth, British monkey butler voice.

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:43.510
Ok, so now I'm going to play the clip

00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:45.350
Will you marry me?

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:46.180
That's what it sounds like have you heard it?

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:47.480
Yes.

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:50.780
So, your idea of what a monkey voice sounded like--

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:53.600
it wasn't creepy, beyond the grave--type

00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:56.100
marriage proposal, like we just heard.

00:00:56.110 --> 00:00:58.559
Why does this sound the way it does?

00:00:58.559 --> 00:01:01.799
Where did the research team come up with this voice?

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:05.460
This all goes back to this question of

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:06.860
why can't our closest primate relatives --

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:09.540
chimps, bonobos, even monkeys

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:10.770
-- why can't they talk?

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:12.870
And for a long time, scientists thought

00:01:12.870 --> 00:01:14.420
well maybe they just don't have the

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:16.140
right vocal anatomy to talk.

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:18.060
Maybe their vocal cords aren't arranged

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:19.700
in the right way or whatever.

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:23.180
But that isn't satisfying --

00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:24.400
especially when you consider

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:27.660
that animals like parrots and elephants can

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:29.940
make noises actually very similar to human speech

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.180
and they have a radically different anatomy than we do

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:35.600
and that a lot of our primate relatives do.

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:37.840
So the alternate explanation is that their brains

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:39.720
aren't controlling their physiology

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:41.820
the way our brains control our physiology.

00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:42.900
Right.

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:46.800
The researchers tried to figure out which was the case.

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:51.180
They turned to a long-tail macaque named Emiliano

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:53.520
and they didn't ask him to talk.

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:55.600
but what they did do is they had him sit in a chair

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:58.120
and they asked him to do a bunch of different things like:

00:01:58.280 --> 00:02:04.380
eat, yawn, a variety of vocalizations, and lip smacks.

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:06.140
While he was doing this

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:07.679
they shot x-ray video of him.

00:02:07.679 --> 00:02:10.379
And then what they did with this x-ray video

00:02:10.379 --> 00:02:11.819
is they actually used it to reconstruct his

00:02:11.819 --> 00:02:12.819
vocal anatomy.

00:02:12.819 --> 00:02:14.780
How he created sounds.

00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:17.140
And then they fed this into a computer program

00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:19.940
and they had this computer program

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:22.580
say the audio that you just played:

00:02:22.940 --> 00:02:23.960
Will you marry me?

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:27.360
Based on that they concluded that yes

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:28.740
monkeys theoretically could talk like people.

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:30.280
Right.

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:31.880
I listened to the human version of this.

00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:33.579
so they did something similar with people.

00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:34.700
I'll play it:

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:36.100
Will you marry me?

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:37.480
It doesn't sound that much different.

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:41.580
So this isn't really saying monkeys sound like --

00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:42.740
ghostly voices.

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:44.820
It's just --this is the best the computer simulation

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:46.340
can do with those components?

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:47.300
Exactly.

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:48.980
Fundamentally what it's showing

00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:49.860
in the very least -

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:52.440
we can argue about the quality of the recording

00:02:52.460 --> 00:02:54.100
but it shows that at least monkeys could

00:02:54.260 --> 00:02:56.160
theoretically produce these types of sounds.

00:02:56.260 --> 00:02:57.520
And if heard them,

00:02:57.600 --> 00:02:59.900
we would be able to understand what they were saying.

